# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
aristotle-politics-1.md:1: argue/readings: S1 is read 2 ways: aristotle-politics-1.logic.r1 and aristotle-politics-1.logic.r1.3 have "same_rank_in_office" and "same_rank_in_office → causes_sedition" and "causes_sedition → magistracy_hazardous" and "golden_particle_fixed → same_rank_in_office" and "military_unhappy" and "all_citizens_happy_aim" and "city_happy → part_happy"; aristotle-politics-1.logic.r1.2 has "same_rank_in_office" and "same_rank_in_office → causes_sedition" and "causes_sedition → magistracy_hazardous" and "golden_particle_fixed" and "golden_particle_fixed → same_rank_in_office" and "military_unhappy" and "city_happy → part_happy"; they differ when only "Socrates would have persons of the same rank always in office", "this becomes the cause of sedition, especially among the courageous and warlike", "settling the magistracy as Socrates has done is very hazardous", "Socrates deprives the military of happiness", "all, or the greater, or some part of the city is happy", "the whole city is happy" and "Socrates says the legislator ought to make all the citizens happy" are true, which the first allows
aristotle-politics-1.md:1: argue/answered: S1: answered no, which keeps aristotle-politics-1.logic.r1.2, to: Suppose "Socrates would have persons of the same rank always in office", "this becomes the cause of sedition, especially among the courageous and warlike", "settling the magistracy as Socrates has done is very hazardous", "Socrates deprives the military of happiness", "all, or the greater, or some part of the city is happy", "the whole city is happy" and "Socrates says the legislator ought to make all the citizens happy" are true and "the golden particle God mixed into the soul does not pass from one to another but stays with the same persons" is false. Is S1, "It is also very hazardous to settle the magistracy as Socrates has done; for he would have persons of the same rank always in office, which becomes the cause of sedition even amongst those who are of no account, but more particularly amongst those who are of a courageous and warlike disposition; it is indeed evidently necessary that he should frame his community in this manner; for that golden particle which God has mixed up in the soul of man flies not from one to the other, but always continues with the same; for he says, that some of our species have gold, and others silver, blended in their composition from the moment of their birth: but those who are to be husbandmen and artists, brass and iron; besides, though he deprives the military of happiness, he says, that the legislator ought to make all the citizens happy; but it is impossible that the whole city can be happy, without all, or the greater, or some part of it be happy.", true then?
aristotle-politics-1.logic.r1: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 2 ways: aristotle-politics-1.logic.r1 and aristotle-politics-1.logic.r1.3 have "magistracy_hazardous ∧ all_citizens_happy_aim ∧ ¬city_happy → has_defects"; aristotle-politics-1.logic.r1.2 has "magistracy_hazardous ∧ ¬city_happy → has_defects"; they differ when only "settling the magistracy as Socrates has done is very hazardous" is true, which the first allows
